# A DOUBLE RELEASE, and it is the most serious thing this counter catches
# (M-marked-acquisition, 2026-09-14). The binding below is entirely correct:
# `slot_open` says `acquires slot_close`, `slot_close` says `consumes`, and
# `check/acquiring.hero` is satisfied. The PROGRAM hands the same handle back
# twice, so the count goes to -1 and the exit says so.
#
# **This case exists because the message was wrong for it.** Until this day the
# negative sentence named exactly one cause, *a `consumes` whose producer
# carries no mark*, which was the only one there was when the counter landed.
# Run against this program it accused the declaration, sending its reader to
# edit an `extern` while the program was releasing twice. Three causes were run
# that afternoon and the message named one; it now names all three, worst
# first, and this is the worst.
#
# Why the counter and not a rule: `selfhost/check/leasing.hero:29` and
# `selfhost/check/consuming.hero:22` both state that the checker has no flow
# analysis, and *how many times does this path run* is a flow question.

extern "abort-handle-given-back-twice.h"
    record Slot tag Slot
    function slot_open(n: i64) -> Slot acquires slot_close
    function slot_close(s: Slot consumes)
    function slot_value(s: Slot) -> i64

function main()
    a = slot_open(n: 7)
    print(slot_value(s: a))
    slot_close(a)
    slot_close(a)
